<h2>A trial that stopped moving</h2>
<p>The prosecution dates from 2018; the Special Court trial has been pending since 2019. Ten prosecution witnesses were examined between 2019 and 2025 — and then the case stalled. The petitioner, who had been on bail, failed to appear; a non-bailable warrant issued on 12 February 2025; he was arrested and remanded on 17 August 2026. By the hearing date he had spent roughly two months and three weeks back in custody, with the case posted for PWs 13 to 17 just two days later.</p>
<p>The State&rsquo;s opposition rested on the obvious point: a man who jumped bail once has forfeited the court&rsquo;s confidence. The defence&rsquo;s answer was equally direct: the trial had made no progress since 2024, and custody was running while nothing happened.</p>

<h2>How the Court resolved it</h2>
<p>Justice Ramesh declined to discuss the merits of the allegations at all. The operative considerations were structural: bail had earlier been granted, so the threshold assessment had already been made once; the NBW was executed promptly; the chargesheet had long been filed; and the petitioner had already served a period of incarceration after rearrest. Against the real risk of flight, the Court deployed conditions rather than continued custody.</p>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><strong>1. Security.</strong> A bond of Rs 25,000 with two sureties, verified on Judicial Form No. 46 with photographs and thumb impressions.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>2. Daily reporting.</strong> Appearance before the trial court every day at 10:30 a.m. until further orders — an unusually strict condition, calibrated to the earlier absconding.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>3. Breach machinery.</strong> On violation, the trial court may act as if it had itself granted bail, on the authority of <em>P.K. Shaji v. State of Kerala</em>; fresh absconding may also attract prosecution under Section 269 BNS as recorded in the order.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>4. The deadline.</strong> The Special Court must expedite and complete the trial within four months of receiving the order.</div>
</div>

<h2>Why the order matters beyond its facts</h2>
<p>Successive bail applications require a change in circumstances, and this order illustrates the cleanest form of one: a prosecution that stops examining witnesses while the accused is in custody. The judgment also shows the modern judicial toolkit for flight risk. Twenty years ago, an executed NBW would likely have ended the bail inquiry. Today, courts increasingly treat custody as the last resort even for a defaulter — provided conditions can realistically secure attendance. Daily reporting at the trial court is about as stringent as conditions come, and it has a self-enforcing elegance: the accused must be physically present at the very forum whose proceedings he once avoided.</p>
<div class="note">The four-month completion direction binds the prosecution as much as the accused. If the deadline passes without judgment, the stagnation argument returns with greater force — and the daily-reporting condition itself may be ripe for relaxation on application.</div>

<h2>A Delhi practice footnote</h2>
<p>The same architecture is available in Delhi: Section 483 BNSS bail before the Sessions Court or High Court, conditions under Section 480(3), and trial-expedition directions where custody and stagnation coexist. An applicant with a default history should come prepared to propose strict conditions in terms — courts respond better to a concrete attendance plan than to assurances.</p>
